Still, this is a misleading statement, because the calculation is 
neither a simple addition nor a simple multiplication.  First, it should 
be said, that as long as the probabilities are small enough, addition 
gives an approximately correct result here.  Chance for each wand: 
1/200*1/5*1/3=1/3000, chance for three wands ~ 1/1000.

The precise result is that of Dylan and it can be calculated this way
(easily):  Chance for Mjollnir hitting you and targeting wands: 
1/200*1/5=1/1000. Chance for each wand to explode after that: 1/3. 
Chance for none out of n wands to be destroyed (1-1/3)^n, for n=3: 
2/3^3=8/27.  So chance for at least one to be destroyed: 
(1-(2/3)^3)*1/1000=19/27*1/1000.  This is of the same order of magnitude 
but different from 1/1000, as 1-(1-1/3)^n is not really well 
approximated by n*1/3, 1/3 not being very small. For a probability p<<1 
instead of 1/3, we would however have (1-(1-p)^n) ~ n p.

> CMIIW, but I think this depends on the number of wands/rings.

Sure, the formula for general n is (1-(2/3)^n)/1000 = (n*1/3- 
n*(n-1)/2*1/9 + n*(n-1)*(n-2)/6*1/27 -+...)/1000

